moveoff.comp is a 'Hal-only' method for providing offsets for up to nine axes (x,y,z,a,b,c,u,v,w).

The moveoff.comp component provides min, max, velocity, and acceleration limits for each axis (similar to the limit3 component).

See the manpage for more information:

  $ man moveoff

The demonstration configs show:

  o moveoff_gui
    REMOVED. It was a Tk panel on the deleted Tcl HAL binding, and it also
    did two things that were not display -- see the note at the end of this
    file.

  o hookup_moveoff.tcl
    a script to connect a moveoff component

  o moveoff.halscope
    halscope configs to illustrate movement
    (GUI's to not show Hal-only offset movements)

Demonstration configs:

  demonstration    mode    controls
  ---------------- ------- -------------------- 
1 onpause_local    onpause local
2 onpause_external onpause external (simulated)
3 always_local     always  local
4 always_external  always  external (simulated)
5 no_gui           NA      NA
6 zretract         onpause pyvcp panel
7 pyvcp            onpause pyvcp panel
8 gladevcp         onpause gladevcp panel


moveoff_gui removal
-------------------

moveoff_gui is gone with the rest of the Tcl HAL tooling. Replacing it is
config work only -- the moveoff component already does everything needed, and
no new component is required. It has NOT been done here because every config
in this directory loads a .tcl HALFILE (LIB:basic_sim.tcl,
LIB:hookup_moveoff.tcl) and those are rejected at startup, so none of them run
and a replacement could not be verified. Do this as part of the .tcl HALFILE
conversion, when these configs can start again:

1. The startup wiring. moveoff_gui made these connections from Tcl at run time
   (connect_pin_to_sig) rather than from a HAL file, which is why 7_pyvcp had
   to keep running it with -no_display. The component's own pin documentation
   says where power-on belongs:

     net mvoff_gui:power_on      motion.motion-enabled   => mv.power-on
     net mvoff_gui:apply_offsets halui.program.is-paused => mv.apply-offsets

   Keep those signal names: moveoff_display_7.hal and moveoff_display_8.hal
   reference mvoff_gui:apply_offsets.

2. Auto-resume (`-autoresume -delay N`). moveoff_gui polled the pins from a Tcl
   timer; in HAL it composes from shipped components -- not, and2, timedelay,
   oneshot. The sequence it implemented (see request_resume_after_delay in the
   old script) was:

     move-enable released while apply-offsets is TRUE
       -> wait for offset-applied to go FALSE (component has returned to the
          un-offsetted position)
       -> wait `delay` seconds
       -> pulse halui.program.resume

   Both cancel conditions -- move-enable coming back, or something else
   resuming the program (is-paused dropping) -- fall out of the AND chain,
   because timedelay restarts its on-delay whenever its input goes false. No
   separate cancel path is needed.

   Watch pin directions when wiring this: move-enable is an IN pin driven by
   the panel, so the auto-resume chain reads the signal that drives it, not the
   pin itself.

3. The display half needs nothing new: moveoff_display_7.xml is an existing
   pyvcp panel covering the same controls, and 8_gladevcp has a gladevcp one.
